 details  that a company or an organization might  otherwise  choose to keep secret or keep hidden or  limited to a certain number  of executives or leaders at a company  netflix is one of the companies best  known for its culture of radical  transparency  that it says promotes accountability  netflix the company takes radical  transparency very far and they go so far  as to have  you know public discussions or public  emails sent out to  you know sometimes hundreds of employees  discussing why someone was fired  sometimes the discussions about why  someone is getting fired happen  right in front of them so that they have  a chance to share all the ways  that they made mistakes that led to them  no longer working at the company  but they also have policies that people  might see as pretty welcome so for  example  they allow their employees to  sort of at their discretion make use of  their expense accounts so that means  no one's really tracking their vacation  days or their expense spending  or or things that are you know useful to  this person getting their job done and  the idea is that  it's freedom and and sort of freedom and  responsibility  that is given to these employees so that  they have a chance  to do their job the best they can  without being micromanaged  but you only have to look at netflix on  glassdoor  to see that this approach isn't for  everyone  anytime you have a company culture  that's sort of extreme  you have the chance to attract people  for whom it's really a good fit  and you're also going to turn off a lot  of people who just feel that this isn't  for them and it may explain why this  isn't  a more widely adopted thing because it's  difficult and it requires someone at the  top  really committed to working through the  uncomfortable parts of it  in order to make it the company culture  for netflix that person at the top is  reed hastings  he believes the company's success stems  from its high performance  culture in the traditional industrial  paradigm  you know you have to do something wrong  to get let go  you can think of a job as sort of a  property right you know until you lose  it by abusing your position  but if you think about professional  sports you know if  that team is going to win a championship  it has to have a mix of the right  players that  work well together that are the absolute  best in the world  and so we try to model ourselves like a  professional sports team  so highly paid but you got to earn your  position every year  and it's about performance and you know  that's not  right for everybody some people care  mostly about job security  other people care mostly about excellent  colleagues  and you know playing great team ball  to achieving something important for the  consumers  and the netflix numbers seem to show  that radical transparency is working  while it fires people at a higher rate  than the average u.s  company people actually choose to leave  at a much  lower rate but you also don't hear about  the companies for which this didn't work  so well right or the companies that  might have gotten to mid-size and then  things sort of fizzled out  or you know we look at netflix and  bridgewater and we we see these success  stories but we're also not hearing  necessarily the stories where it was  hard to keep a company culture going as  it grew much bigger or it was hard to  keep it going as the company expanded  very quickly  but beyond company's success how does  this high accountability system  impact the average employee so from a  neuroscience perspective  um if we think about what the brain is  in innately  craving like the brain's own goals one  of them is  a sense of certainty like the ability to  predict  what's going to happen the next second  when you feel you know uncertain  it activates quite a strong threat  response in the brain which takes your  attention away  from what you're trying to focus on when  certainty goes  up you activate a reward network in the