r i think that's because it's  probably quite hard to adjust to and and  so you see companies  who really define themselves as a  company culture try to differentiate  themselves from other companies  by emphasizing radical transparency but  those companies in my view remain pretty  few and far between  in addition to the potential legal  issues surrounding employee performance  and termination transparency  a major hurdle for many companies is  making salaries public  yep it's 2021 and women are still being  paid less than men  for doing the same jobs one  step in the direction of of amending  this  this reality is to have pay  transparency in the case of paid  transparency  there's this pretty strong argument that  actually hiding  pay between your employees can hold  people back  it lets certain employees get paid more  and certain employees go underpaid for a  long time without knowing  and so there's i think a pretty strong  argument to be made  that you know pay transparency is more  fair  a 2021 washington university study  found that pay secrecy directly  contributes to the continued pay  inequality  between men and women doing the same  role  i wouldn't be surprised if certain of  the more palatable  or easy to digest changes that come with  radical transparency say maybe like  pay transparency i think that's  something that more companies  could get on board with adopting it's  less of a culture shock than  changing the way that you do all sorts  of feedback or firings or meetings or  sensitive things like that  and if that becomes more and more common  then you'll see  companies who want to distinguish  themselves thinking about  well what's next like what is something  else that we can be changing about how  we run our business that's going to make  us  better and different from our  competitors  a year of remote working has challenged  us to rethink  many of our traditional work models  and while radical transparency in its  most extreme  form may not b